  "problem": "In a plumbing store, the length of a water pipe is normally distributed with a mean of $2\\,\\text{m}$ and a standard deviation of $0.05\\,\\text{m}.$ If Carl bought $25$ pipes, what is the probability that the total length of the pipes he purchased is more than $49.8\\,m$ and less $50.2\\,m?$ It can be assumed that the lengths of pipes are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |",
